ox-crt - README

Copyright (C) 2026 OXlatorty

This project is under Apache License 2.0
There is own implementation of C RUNTIME (CRT).

=============================================================[ Project Info ]=============================================================

Modules:
  - ox-crt-defines <-- defining uintptr_t without stdint.h
  - ox-crt-syscall <-- syscalls from 1 arg to 5, in C and __asm__ volatile
  - ox-crt-start   <-- entry point (main() caller), in C and __asm__

Build:
  - Linux x86_64:
      -> ./make-all.x86_64.sh
      )> or directly: make -f "Makefiles/Makefile.ox-crt.posix.x86_64.mak" all
  - Windows x86_64
      -> make-all.x86_64.bat
      )> or directly: make -f "Makefiles/Makefile.ox-crt.windows.x86_64.mak" all
  - Linux x86
      -> ./make-all.x86.sh
      )> or directly: make -f "Makefiles/Makefile.ox-crt.posix.x86.mak" all
  - Windows x86
      -> make-all.x86.bat
      )> or directly: make -f "Makefiles/Makefile.ox-crt.windows.x86.mak" all

  Out files should be in obj/ directory

Supported Platforms:
  - Posix x86
  - Posix x86_64
  - Windows x86
  - Windows x86_64

ATTENTION FOR WINDOWS USERS: 
  Windows doesn't have stable kernel ABI, this is why you should correct syscalls in start func for your Windows version.

=============================================================[ Usage Example ]=============================================================

First copy compiled (OX-)CRT from obj/ into your workspace folder.

For Posix x86 or x86_64 create main.c file

```
int main(int argc, char** argv, char** envp) {
  return 42; 
}
```

For Windows x86 or x86_64 create main.c file

```
int main(int argc, char** argv) {
  return 42;
}
```

ATTENTION: There is 42 for example, you can change it on your own number to check later.

Compilation for Posix x86

```
clang -m32 -c main.c -o main.o -DOX_CRT_POSIX_X86 -ffreestanding -fno-builtin
clang -m32 -nostdlib -e ox_crt_start main.o ox-crt-start.o ox-crt-syscall.o -o my_program
```

Compilation for Posix x86_64

```
clang -c main.c -o main.o -DOX_CRT_POSIX_X86_64 -ffreestanding -fno-builtin
clang -nostdlib -e ox_crt_start main.o ox-crt-start.o ox-crt-syscall.o -o my_program
```

Compilation for Windows x86

```
clang --target=x86-pc-windows-msvc -c main.c -o main.obj -DOX_CRT_WINDOWS_X86 -ffreestanding -fno-builtin
clang --target=x86-pc-windows-msvc -nostdlib -Wl,/entry:ox_crt_start main.obj ox-crt-start.o ox-crt-syscall.o -o my_program.exe
```

Compilation for Windows x86_64

```
clang --target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc -c main.c -o main.obj -DOX_CRT_WINDOWS_X86_64 -ffreestanding -fno-builtin
clang --target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc -nostdlib -Wl,/entry:ox_crt_start main.obj ox-crt-start.o ox-crt-syscall.o -o my_program.exe
```

Test run on Posix x86 or x86_64

```
./my_program
echo $?
```

Test run on Windows x86 or x86_64

```
my_program.exe
echo %ERRORLEVEL%
```

Should be return 42 or your specific number.

And it is all, you successfully ran C code without libc.

